course: dictionary
lesson: 5092
title: Intensive: hard work or constant care
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Intensive means using a lot of effort or work in a short time. An «intensive course» packs many lessons into a few days. The clues are the words around it: course, short time, lots of work.
explain: In hospitals, intensive care is a special ward. «Intensive care is a special hospital ward where very poorly or very early babies are watched day and night.» The clue is watched day and night: this means serious, constant care, not a quick check-up.
explain: Intensive is also used in farming. Intensive farming uses lots of machines, chemicals or animals in a small space to make more food. Test any meaning by putting it into the sentence in place of the hard word. If it still makes sense, you probably have it right.
fact: Intensive means using a lot of effort or work in a short time.
fact: Intensive care is a part of a hospital for very ill people who need constant care and special machines.
fact: Intensive farming uses lots of machines, chemicals or animals in a small space to produce more food.
fact: The adverb is intensively, which means in an intensive way.
fact: Context clues such as «day and night» or «a short time» can help you work out what intensive means.
exercise: «Intensive care is a special hospital ward where very poorly or very early babies are watched day and night.» What is intensive care? | *A part of a hospital for very ill people who need constant care | A shop that sells baby clothes | A hospital car park | A school for doctors
exercise: Mia is taking an intensive course in swimming for one week. What does intensive suggest? | Very cheap and easy | Slow and lazy | Held outside in the park | *Lots of effort packed into a short time
exercise: Which phrase means many machines, chemicals or animals in a small space to make more food? | Hospital ward | Geography homework | Short course | *Intensive farming
test: Which word means using a lot of effort or work in a short time? | Gentle | *Intensive | Lazy | Spare
test: Dad says the hospital ward is for very poorly babies who are watched day and night. What is the ward? | A shop for baby clothes | A quiet garden | A school for babies | *Intensive care
exam: Which sentence uses intensive correctly? | *He joined an intensive training camp to get fit fast. | We walked to the shops on Sunday. | The cat slept on the rug all day. | She ate a sandwich on the bus.
